data.content:
**Yes, C# fully supports `stackalloc`'d `Span<nint>` and `Span<nuint>`.** Both work exactly as you'd expect:

```csharp
Span<nint>  a = stackalloc nint[5];
Span<nuint> b = stackalloc nuint[3];
```

On this 64-bit system each `nint` is 8 bytes. The elements are indexable, assignable, and behave like any other `Span<T>` backed by stack memory. This has been supported since C# 9 / .NET 5 when `nint`/`nuint` were introduced as first-class types.
